{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c41b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae0f-ee07-7b81-b4d4-cf94ccb5debd"},"sequence":104,"recorded_at":1780531400000203,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b7392d99-bbd5-4114-ba32-61509405cfc4","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b7392d99-bbd5-4114-ba32-61509405cfc4","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b7392d99-bbd5-4114-ba32-61509405cfc4"},"call_id":"call_01a0ae10193f74a8b4d215447402515b","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: go-testing-tools\n3|description: >\n4|  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n5|  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n6|  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\n7|triggers:\n8|  - go test\n9|  - bubbletea test\n10|  - tuitestkit\n11|  - go test helpers\n12|  - reducer test\n13|  - snapshot test go\n14|  - golden file go\n15|  - тесты го\n16|  - го тест хелперы\n17|  - тесты баблти\n18|  - тест утилиты го\n19|  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n20|---\n21|\n22|# Go Testing Tools\n23|\n24|Testing toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n25|\n26|Core philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n27|\n28|---\n29|\n30|## Prerequisites\n31|\n32|- **Go 1.21+**\n33|- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n34|- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n35|\n36|Install the library in your project:\n37|\n38|```bash\n39|go get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n40|```\n41|\n42|---\n43|\n44|## Quick Start\n45|\n46|Minimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n47|\n48|```go\n49|package myscreen_test\n50|\n51|import (\n52|    \"testing\"\n53|\n54|    \"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\"\n55|)\n56|\n57|func TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n58|    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n59|    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n60|\n61|    // Press down twice\n62|    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"), tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"))\n63|\n64|    // Assert the view contains expected content\n65|    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \"third item\")\n66|    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \"ERROR\")\n67|}\n68|```\n69|\n70|---\n71|\n72|## Testing Pyramid\n73|\n74|Five levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n75|\n76|| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n77||-------|------|-------|------------|\n78|| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n79|| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n80|| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n81|| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n82|| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n83|\n84|When time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n85|\n86|Full details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n87|\n88|---\n89|\n90|## Library API Reference\n91|\n92|All functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n93|\n94|### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n95|\n96|Build `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n97|\n98|```go\n99|// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n100|// Supports: \"enter\", \"tab\", \"esc\", \"space\", \"backspace\", \"up\", \"down\",\n101|// \"left\", \"right\", \"home\", \"end\", \"pgup\", \"pgdown\", \"delete\", \"insert\",\n102|// \"f1\"-\"f20\", \"ctrl+c\", \"ctrl+a\"...\"ctrl+z\", \"alt+h\", \"alt+enter\",\n103|// single runes: \"a\", \"/\", \"1\", etc.\n104|func Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n105|\n106|// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\n107|func Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n108|\n109|// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\n110|func WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n111|\n112|// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\n113|func MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n114|\n115|// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\n116|func MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n117|\n118|// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n119|// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\n120|func MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n121|\n122|// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\n123|func MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n124|```\n125|\n126|### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n127|\n128|Send messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n129|\n130|```go\n131|// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n132|// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n133|// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n134|// Init() is NOT called.\n135|func Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n136|\n137|// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n138|// Returns final model + collected commands.\n139|func SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n140|\n141|// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n142|// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\n143|func ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n144|```\n145|\n146|**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n147|\n148|```go\n149|m, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"r\"))\n150|msgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\n151|m = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n152|```\n153|\n154|### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n155|\n156|Table-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n157|\n158|```go\n159|// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n160|// S = state type, A = action type.\n161|type ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n162|    Name    string\n163|    Initial S\n164|    Action  A\n165|    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n166|}\n167|\n168|// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n169|// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\n170|type Step[S any, A any] struct {\n171|    Name   string\n172|    Action A\n173|    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n174|}\n175|\n176|// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n177|// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\n178|type ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n179|    Name    string\n180|    Initial S\n181|    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n182|    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n183|}\n184|\n185|// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\n186|func RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n187|\n188|// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n189|// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\n190|func RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n191|```\n192|\n193|**Invariant checking:**\n194|\n195|```go\n196|// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\n197|type Invariant[S any] struct {\n198|    Name  string\n199|    Check func(s S) error\n200|}\n201|\n202|// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\n203|type InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n204|\n205|// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\n206|func NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n207|\n208|// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\n209|func (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n210|\n211|// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n212|// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n213|// t.Fatalf on violation.\n214|func WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n215|```\n216|\n217|**Example:**\n218|\n219|```go\n220|checker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n221|    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n222|        Name: \"cursor in bounds\",\n223|        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n224|            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\n225|                return fmt.Errorf(\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n226|            }\n227|            return nil\n228|        },\n229|    },\n230|)\n231|safeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\n232|tuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n233|```\n234|\n235|### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n236|\n237|Composable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n238|\n239|```go\n240|// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\n241|type MockCall struct {\n242|    Method string\n243|    Args   []any\n244|}\n245|\n246|// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n247|// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\n248|type MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n249|\n250|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\n251|func (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\n252|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\n253|func (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\n254|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n255|\n256|// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\n257|type MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n258|\n259|func NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\n260|func (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\n261|func (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\n262|func (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n263|\n264|// Assertion helpers\n265|func AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\n266|func AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\n267|func AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\n268|func AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n269|```\n270|\n271|**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n272|\n273|```go\n274|type TaskBoardMock struct {\n275|    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n276|    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n277|}\n278|\n279|func NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n280|    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n281|}\n282|\n283|func (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n284|    m.Record(\"TreeJSON\")\n285|    return m.Responses.Get(\"TreeJSON\")\n286|}\n287|\n288|func (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n289|    m.Record(\"Execute\", cmd, args)\n290|    return m.Responses.Get(\"Execute:\" + cmd)\n291|}\n292|```\n293|\n294|### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n295|\n296|Assert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n297|\n298|```go\n299|// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\n300|func StripANSI(s string) string\n301|\n302|// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n303|\n304|func ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\n305|func ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\n306|func ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\n307|func ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\n308|func ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\n309|func ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n310|\n311|// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n312|\n313|func ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\n314|func NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\n315|func LinesFromStr(view string) []string\n316|func MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n317|```\n318|\n319|### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n320|\n321|Golden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n322|\n323|```go\n324|// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n325|// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\n326|var UpdateSnapshots bool\n327|\n328|// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\n329|func SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n330|\n331|// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\n332|func SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n333|\n334|// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\n335|func SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n336|\n337|// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\n338|func SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n339|```\n340|\n341|Golden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/<name>.golden` relative to the test file.\n342|\n343|```bash\n344|# Create or update golden files\n345|UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n346|\n347|# Update for a single package\n348|UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n349|```\n350|\n351|Mismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n352|\n353|---\n354|\n355|## Closed-Loop Workflow\n356|\n357|The agent development cycle for writing tests:\n358|\n359|```\n360|1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n361|2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n362|3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n363|4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n364|5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n365|6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n366|7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n367|8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n368|9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n369|```\n370|\n371|### Step Details\n372|\n373|**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n374|- What state does it manage?\n375|- What messages does it handle in Update?\n376|- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n377|- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n378|- What does View() render?\n379|\n380|**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n381|- Pure state logic -> Level 1 (reducer tests)\n382|- Key bindings, view rendering -> Level 2 (component tests)\n383|- Data loading, command execution -> Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n384|- Visual appearance -> Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n385|\n386|**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n387|1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n388|2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n389|3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n390|4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n391|\n392|**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n393|- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n394|- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n395|- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n396|- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n397|\n398|**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n399|\n400|**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n401|\n402|**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n403|- Type assertion panic -> `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n404|- View assertion failure -> check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n405|- Snapshot mismatch -> run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n406|- Mock assertion failure -> check response key matches (`\"Execute:list\"` not `\"Execute\"`)\n407|\n408|---\n409|\n410|## Templates\n411|\n412|Scaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n413|\n414|| Template | Purpose |\n415||----------|---------|\n416|| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n417|| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n418|| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n419|| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n420|| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n421|| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n422|\n423|Copy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n424|\n425|---\n426|\n427|## References\n428|\n429|In-depth documentation in `references/`:\n430|\n431|| Document | Content |\n432||----------|---------|\n433|| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n434|| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n435|| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n436|\n437|---\n438|\n439|## Setup\n440|\n441|Run the setup script to create symlinks:\n442|\n443|```bash\n444|./scripts/setup.sh\n445|```\n446|\n447|This links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n448|\n449|To remove:\n450|\n451|```bash\n452|./scripts/deinit.sh\n453|```","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
